# Quick Reference - Commands, Checklists & Troubleshooting

> Setting a packet's session goal? The shape of what you type — a pointer plus the
> completion criteria copied out — is in [goal-set-string-playbook.md](./goal-set-string-playbook.md).
> Writing or amending a `goal.md`, including a phase parent's binding table? Use
> `/create:goal` from `sk-create-goal`.

Fast lookup for spec folder commands, checklists, and troubleshooting.

---

## 1. OVERVIEW

### Purpose

Quick reference for spec folder management, commands, and troubleshooting using the progressive enhancement model.

### Progressive Enhancement Model

```
Level 1 (Baseline):     spec.md + plan.md + tasks.md + implementation-summary.md
Level 2 (Verification): Level 1 + acceptance-criteria.md
Level 3 (Full):         Level 2 + optional decision-record.md + optional research
Level 3+ (Extended):    Level 3 + governance/AI execution content
```

### Key Points

- LOC thresholds are **SOFT GUIDANCE** (not enforcement)
- **Enforcement is MANUAL** - verify required templates exist before claiming completion

---

## 2. LEVEL DECISION SHORTCUTS (PROGRESSIVE ENHANCEMENT)

| Situation | Level | Required Templates | Optional Templates |
|-----------|-------|-------------------|-------------------|
| Any task (baseline) | 1 | spec.md + plan.md + tasks.md + implementation-summary.md | timeline.md (any level) |
| Needs QA validation | 2 | L1 + acceptance-criteria.md | timeline.md (any level) |
| Complex/architectural | 3 | L2 file set | decision-record.md, research/research.md, timeline.md |
| Enterprise/governance heavy | 3+ | L3 file set rendered at Level 3+ | research/research.md, timeline.md |

## 1. OVERVIEW AND BOUNDS

You write one packet, in one folder. The folder is the one `create.sh` prints.

Allowed writes: the packet folder and its `scratch/` folder.
Forbidden: `git commit`, `git push`, `git checkout`, `git switch`, `git branch`, `git reset`, `git stash`, `generate-context.js`, `/speckit:save`. Do not edit `.skilled/`, another packet or a source file. Do not run `create.sh` twice.

Acceptance: you can name the packet folder and the one command you will run to check it.
Verify: `pwd` prints the repository root, and `ls <packet folder>` lists `spec.md`.

---
## 8. FILL EVERY TOKEN

A scaffold stamped `v2.3` or later carries tokens. A token starts with `[YOUR_VALUE_HERE:` and ends at the next closing bracket. Replace the whole token, brackets included, with real content. Do not leave a token in a finished packet.

If you cannot answer a question the token asks, write `UNKNOWN` in its place and write an open question in `goal.md` section 4.

A token in a packet whose `spec.md` says `Status: Draft` is a warning, so strict validation can pass with tokens still in the file. A token in any other status is an error. Do not use the warning as permission. Count the tokens.

Acceptance: the count is 0.
Verify: `rg -o '\[YOUR_VALUE_HERE:' <packet folder>/*.md | wc -l` prints 0.

---

## 9. VALIDATE

Run the validator once, after your last edit, before you claim the packet is done.

```bash
bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <packet folder> --strict --quiet
```

It prints one `RESULT:` line per packet; the first line is the folder you named, and that line decides. `RESULT: PASSED (errors=0 ...)` is the pass. Do not run it again to re-check a pass.

When it prints `FAILED`, run the same command once without `--quiet` and read every failing rule. Fix all of them in one pass, then run the `--quiet` command again. That is one round. Stop after three rounds and write the rules and their errors in the packet's `goal.md`.

When the full output contains a `repair-derived.cjs ... --apply` command, run that command, then run the `--quiet` command again. It rewrites generated metadata, frontmatter packet pointers and the Spec Folder row, never your prose.

When `spec.md` says `Status: Draft` and every error is a derived-metadata one, a `--strict` validate run repairs that metadata itself: it applies `repair-derived.cjs --apply` once, prints each file it rewrote and validates again. It does not run when `CI` is set or `SPECKIT_RULES` names a subset, and `SPECKIT_DRAFT_AUTO_REPAIR=0` turns it off.

Acceptance: the `--quiet` command prints `RESULT: PASSED`.
Verify: the last `--quiet` run printed `RESULT: PASSED`.
## 1. OVERVIEW

Path-scoped rules enable differentiated validation based on:
- File location (scratch/, memory/, templates/)
- Documentation level (1/2/3/3+/phase parent)
- File type (spec.md, decision-record.md, etc.)

### Why This Matters

| Scenario                | Without Scoping        | With Scoping                   |
| ----------------------- | ---------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| scratch/ prototypes     | Full validation blocks | Skipped entirely               |
| Level 3 decision record | Same as notes          | ADR-specific checks            |
| Template files          | Placeholder errors     | Exempt from content validation |

---

## 2. RULE HIERARCHY

```
GLOBAL RULES (always apply)
    ↓
LEVEL RULES (by documentation level)
    ↓
PATH RULES (by file pattern)
    ↓
ENVIRONMENT OVERRIDES (runtime control)
```

---

## 3. PATH PATTERNS

### By Directory

| Pattern                                        | Behavior                     |
| ---------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| `**/scratch/**`                                | Skip all validation          |
| `**/memory/**`                                 | Minimal validation (generated continuity support artifacts validated by ANCHORS_VALID only) |
| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/**` | Skip content validation      |
| `specs/**/`                                    | Level-appropriate validation (recursive through nested packet families) |
| `<active-spec-folder>/**/`                          | Level-appropriate validation (recursive through nested packet families) |

### By Level

| Level | Required Files               | Checks Applied                            |
| ----- | ---------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| 1     | spec.md, plan.md, tasks.md   | FILE_EXISTS, PLACEHOLDER_FILLED, SECTIONS |
| 2     | Level 1 + acceptance-criteria.md       | + P0/P1 section headers                   |
| 3     | Level 2 files (decision-record.md is a lazy add-on, not required) | + Context/Decision/Consequences sections  |

---

